Knowing the patterns isn't enough. Production caches fail in three specific ways, and each requires a different defense.

**Cache stampede** (thundering herd) is the most dangerous. A hot key expires. Simultaneously, hundreds of threads detect the miss and all query the database. The database briefly handles 100× normal load for that single query. I've seen this cascade into full database failure when five hot keys expired in the same second during a traffic spike.

The fix is a **mutex lock** — only one thread fetches while others wait for the cache to be repopulated. In Java with Redis, this leverages `SETNX` to acquire a short-lived lock so that concurrent threads don't all slam the database at once.

```java
String value = redis.get(key);
if (value == null) {
    boolean acquired = redis.set("lock:" + key, "1",
        SetParams.nx().ex(5));               // 5-second lock TTL
    if (acquired) {
        value = db.fetch(key);               // single thread hits DB
        redis.setex(key, ttlSeconds, value);
        redis.del("lock:" + key);
    } else {
        Thread.sleep(50);                    // wait for lock holder
        value = redis.get(key);              // retry from cache
    }
}
```

The `SETNX` ensures only one thread wins the lock. The 5-second TTL on the lock prevents deadlock if the winning thread crashes. For ultra-high throughput keys, **probabilistic early refresh** works better — each access within the last 10% of TTL has a small random chance of triggering a background refresh before the key actually expires, eliminating the miss entirely.

**Cache penetration** happens when requests repeatedly query keys that don't exist in cache *or* database — typically bots probing sequential user IDs. Every request bypasses the cache and hits the database because there's nothing to cache. Two defenses: cache the null result with a short TTL (`redis.setex(key, 60, "NULL")`), or use a **Bloom filter** that checks existence before any lookup. Bloom filters use ~1 byte per key with zero false negatives — if the filter says "doesn't exist," it definitely doesn't.

**Cache avalanche** occurs when many keys expire simultaneously because they were all loaded during the same burst and given identical TTLs. The entire working set vanishes from cache at once, flooding the database. The fix is trivial: **add jitter to TTLs**. Instead of a flat 3600 seconds, use `3600 + random(0, 300)`. Five minutes of jitter spreads the expiration window enough to prevent synchronized misses.

Start by defining the public API. A cache has three operations: `get`, `put`, and `size`. The generic type parameters let callers store any key-value pair. Internally, each entry lives in a `Node` that carries `prev` / `next` pointers for the linked list plus the key (needed during eviction to remove the entry from the map).

```java
public class LRUCache<K, V> {

    private final int capacity;
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<K, Node<K, V>> map;
    private final ReentrantReadWriteLock lock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();

    // Sentinel nodes simplify edge cases — head.next is MRU, tail.prev is LRU
    private final Node<K, V> head = new Node<>(null, null);
    private final Node<K, V> tail = new Node<>(null, null);

    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>(capacity);
        head.next = tail;
        tail.prev = head;
    }

    private static class Node<K, V> {
        final K key;
        volatile V value;  // volatile for safe publication
        Node<K, V> prev;
        Node<K, V> next;

        Node(K key, V value) {
            this.key = key;
            this.value = value;
        }
    }
}
```

Sentinel nodes (`head` and `tail`) eliminate null-checks when inserting or removing at boundaries. The most-recently-used entry always sits at `head.next`; the eviction candidate sits at `tail.prev`. This convention keeps every list operation uniform.

public V get(K key) {
    Node<K, V> node = map.get(key);  // ConcurrentHashMap: lock-free read
    if (node == null) return null;

    // Move to head requires list mutation → write lock
    lock.writeLock().lock();
    try {
        moveToHead(node);
    } finally {
        lock.writeLock().unlock();
    }
    return node.value;
}

public void put(K key, V value) {
    Node<K, V> existing = map.get(key);

    if (existing != null) {
        existing.value = value;  // update in place
        lock.writeLock().lock();
        try {
            moveToHead(existing);
        } finally {
            lock.writeLock().unlock();
        }
        return;
    }

    // New entry
    Node<K, V> newNode = new Node<>(key, value);
    lock.writeLock().lock();
    try {
        addAfterHead(newNode);
        map.put(key, newNode);

        if (map.size() > capacity) {
            Node<K, V> lru = tail.prev;  // least recently used
            removeNode(lru);
            map.remove(lru.key);          // evict from map too
        }
    } finally {
        lock.writeLock().unlock();
    }
}

// ---- linked-list helpers (caller must hold write lock) ----

private void addAfterHead(Node<K, V> node) {
    node.prev = head;
    node.next = head.next;
    head.next.prev = node;
    head.next = node;
}

private void removeNode(Node<K, V> node) {
    node.prev.next = node.next;
    node.next.prev = node.prev;
}

private void moveToHead(Node<K, V> node) {
    removeNode(node);  // unlink from current position
    addAfterHead(node); // re-insert at head
}

public class TTLKeyValueStore<K, V> {

    private final ConcurrentHashMap<K, Entry<V>> store
        = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    private final ScheduledExecutorService cleaner
        = Executors.newSingleThreadScheduledExecutor(r -> {
            Thread t = new Thread(r, "ttl-cleaner");
            t.setDaemon(true);
            return t;
        });

    public TTLKeyValueStore() {
        // Active expiration: sweep every second
        cleaner.scheduleAtFixedRate(
            this::expireRandomSample,
            1, 1, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }

    public void put(K key, V value, long ttlMs) {
        long expiresAt = System.currentTimeMillis() + ttlMs;
        store.put(key, new Entry<>(value, expiresAt));
    }

    public V get(K key) {
        Entry<V> entry = store.get(key);
        if (entry == null) return null;

        // Lazy expiration: check on every read
        if (entry.isExpired()) {
            store.remove(key, entry);  // atomic: only remove this entry
            return null;
        }
        return entry.value;
    }

    public boolean delete(K key) {
        return store.remove(key) != null;
    }

    public int size() {
        return store.size(); // includes expired-but-not-yet-evicted
    }

    // Redis-style probabilistic sweep
    private void expireRandomSample() {
        var keys = store.keySet().toArray();
        if (keys.length == 0) return;

        int sampleSize = Math.min(20, keys.length);
        int expired = 0;
        ThreadLocalRandom rng = ThreadLocalRandom.current();

        for (int i = 0; i < sampleSize; i++) {
            @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
            K key = (K) keys[rng.nextInt(keys.length)];
            Entry<V> entry = store.get(key);
            if (entry != null && entry.isExpired()) {
                store.remove(key, entry);
                expired++;
            }
        }

        // If >25% expired, run again immediately
        if (expired > sampleSize / 4) {
            expireRandomSample(); // recursive burst
        }
    }

    private record Entry<V>(V value, long expiresAt) {
        boolean isExpired() {
            return System.currentTimeMillis() >= expiresAt;
        }
    }
}
